# Project Architecture Rules

The project is a frontend template built with Next.js App Router, React,
Orval-generated REST client and TanStack Query. It follows an adapted Crystal
Architecture focused on layer boundaries, public APIs and client-side
server-state management.

## Layers

Dependency direction is always:

```txt
app → modules → entities → shared
```

- `app/` is a thin Next.js composition layer: routing, layouts, metadata,
  providers, route params, redirects and page composition.
- `modules/` contains base business areas and scenario modules: feature UI,
  query/mutation hooks, scenario rules and local UI state.
- `entities/` contains reusable domain building blocks: schemas, types,
  constants, pure utilities and presentational entity UI.
- `shared/` contains infrastructure: design system, generated API client,
  axios/query client, providers, generic hooks, i18n configs, consts and types.

## Import Boundaries

- A module inside `src/modules/x` may import `src/modules/y` only through
  `@/modules/y` and only for deliberate scenario composition.
- `entities` must not import from `modules` or `app`.
- `shared` must not import from `entities`, `modules` or `app`.
- Any import of another module/entity from outside must go through public API:
  - `@/modules/x`
  - `@/entities/x`
- Direct imports to internal files of another module/entity are forbidden.
- Module dependencies must stay one-way and must not form cycles.
- Run `pnpm lint:deps` to check dependency boundaries.

## Module Structure

Segments are created on demand:

```txt
src/modules/{moduleName}/
├── model/
│   ├── queries/      # useQuery hooks and query keys
│   ├── mutations/    # useMutation hooks, invalidation, optimistic updates
│   ├── stores/       # Zustand ephemeral UI state
│   ├── mappers/      # DTO → domain/view model mapping
│   ├── constants/    # scenario constants
│   └── permissions/  # scenario permissions and checks
├── ui/               # scenario components, split by responsibility zones when needed
├── api/              # optional manual transport adapter only
├── lib/              # pure module-local utilities
└── index.ts          # module public API
```

For a small module, a flatter `model/` is acceptable. When hooks become
noticeable, group by role with `queries/`, `mutations/` and `stores/`. Do not
use `model/hooks` as the default structure because it groups by technical form
instead of architectural role.

## UI Structure

Keep `ui/` flat for small modules. When it grows, split it by responsibility
zones, not by smart/dumb or container/component categories.

Recommended shape:

```txt
ui/
├── ItemDetailsDialog.tsx  # main public scenario component
├── content/
├── table/
├── form/
├── dialog/
├── sheet/
├── drawer/
├── filters/
├── states/
├── toolbar/
└── parts/
```

Rules:

- Keep the main scenario component at the top level of `ui/` when that improves
  discoverability.
- Put smaller pieces into responsibility zones such as `table`, `form`,
  `content`, `states`, `filters`, `toolbar` or overlay-specific folders.
- Use `parts` only for small module-local pieces that do not fit a more precise
  zone.
- Do not create `smart`, `dumb`, `containers`, `components`, `widgets` folders
  as the default split.
- Move reusable presentational pieces used by multiple modules to `entities`
  or `shared`.

## Base Modules And Scenario Modules

- Base modules own one business area: `items`, `activity-log`, `related-records`.
- Scenario modules compose multiple base modules into a user workflow:
  `item-details`, `item-directory`.

Example:

```txt
app/items/page.tsx
↓
modules/item-directory
├── modules/items
└── modules/item-details
    ├── modules/items
    ├── modules/activity-log
    └── modules/related-records
```

Scenario module dependencies are valid when they go through public APIs, stay
one-way, avoid cycles and represent a real user workflow. Do not create a
scenario module for every small component.

## Data Flow

Default data flow:

```txt
UI component
↓
module model query/mutation hook
↓
shared/api/generated
↓
backend
```

Rules:

- Orval output lives in `src/shared/api/generated.ts`.
- Module UI must not import `@/shared/api/generated` directly.
- `app/` routes must not import `@/shared/api/generated` directly.
- Generated API calls are wrapped by hooks/mappers in:
  - `modules/x/model/queries`
  - `modules/x/model/mutations`
  - `modules/x/api` only when a manual transport adapter is needed.
- Do not create module `api/` just to re-export generated functions.
- `modules/x/api` is only for composite requests, uploads/downloads,
  SSE/WebSocket, polling adapters, legacy endpoints or complex transport
  mapping.

## TanStack Query And Zustand

- Server-state from the backend belongs in TanStack Query.
- `useQuery` hooks live in `model/queries`.
- `useMutation` hooks live in `model/mutations`.
- Query keys live next to queries in `model/queries`.
- Invalidation, optimistic updates and mutation-specific mapping live in
  `model/mutations`.
- Zustand stores live in `model/stores` and are only for ephemeral UI state.
- Zustand must not duplicate server data, lists, profiles or details.

Use Zustand for:

- modal/drawer open state;
- selected IDs for sidebars/details;
- wizard steps;
- temporary local drafts before submission.

## Entity Rules

`entities/*` can contain:

- `model/` — zod schemas, TypeScript types, domain constants;
- `lib/` — pure domain utilities;
- `ui/` — presentational entity components;
- `index.ts` — public API.

Entities must not contain API calls, query hooks, mutation hooks, Zustand
stores or feature-specific scenario logic.

## Shared Rules

`shared` must stay infrastructure-only:

```txt
src/shared/
├── api/            # Orval generated client
├── components/ui/  # design system
├── lib/client/     # axios, query client, custom mutation helpers
├── hooks/          # generic hooks
├── providers/      # global providers
├── configs/i18/    # i18n configs
├── consts/         # global constants
└── types/          # global types
```

If code knows business meaning, it does not belong in `shared`.

## Environment Variables

Do not add a shared env facade to this frontend template.

- Browser API base URL: `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` in
  `shared/lib/client/axios-client.ts`.
- OpenAPI source for Orval: `process.env.ORVAL_PATH_URL` in `orval.config.js`.
- Bundle analyzer flag: `process.env.ANALYZE` in `next.config.ts`.

## Public API

Each module/entity exposes only the intended external surface through
`index.ts`.

Example:

```ts
export { UserProfile } from "./ui/UserProfile";
export { useUpdateUser } from "./model/mutations/use-update-user";
export { userKeys } from "./model/queries/user.keys";
```

## Component Coupling

Prefer event callbacks over direct knowledge of parent scenarios.

- A table/list component reports events: `onItemClick(itemId)`.
- A scenario parent decides what the event means: URL update, dialog open,
  navigation, analytics.
- Base components should not know which dialog, sheet or route will react to
  the event.
- For shadcn overlays used from lists/tables, prefer one controlled overlay in
  the scenario parent instead of one overlay instance per row.
